

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Single-user, one browser changes what "notification" can even mean, so I want to say this plainly before we design
  anything:

  [1mIn a solo local app, nobody else ever changes your tasks.[0m You do. And you were sitting right there when you did it —
  telling you "you edited Task 3" a moment after you edited Task 3 is noise, not a notification. The literal request,
  "notify users when tasks they care about change," has no remaining source of change to report.

  What [3mdoes[0m have real value in a solo app is [1mtime passing[0m: a task becomes due, or goes overdue, or a reminder you set
  earlier comes around. That's a genuinely useful notification, but it's a different system than a change-notification
  system — it's driven by a clock and stored due dates, not by an event stream of edits.

  There's also the sequencing reality: [1mthere are still no tasks.[0m Before any notification can fire, this app needs a task
  model, persistence, and a UI to create and edit tasks. That's sub-project #1 and it's most of the work. Notifications
  sit on top of it and should be its own cycle after.

  So the next thing I need from you is what the notification is actually [3mfor[0m.

